<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Windsor (1386)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1386">1386</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Windsor was signed on 9 May 1386 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-ii-of-england">Richard II of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal">John I of Portugal</a>, renewing the alliance of 1373 after English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> had fought for Portugal at Aljubarrota. It pledged perpetual friendship and mutual support, and John's marriage to Philippa of Lancaster in 1387 sealed it. The resulting Anglo-Portuguese Alliance is the oldest diplomatic alliance still in force.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-windsor-1386.jpg" alt="Treaty of Windsor (1386)" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard II of England · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Windsor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
